From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ps5cemu <ps5cemu@localhost>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 06:09:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] PS5: aes_keys.txt with Windows line endings loads

The key file's parsers read it line by line with std::getline, which
keeps a carriage return: in a file saved with Windows line endings the
":AES" section's header read as ":AES\r", which no parser knows, so not
one key was loaded and nothing said so (an amiibo then could not be
written). The stream the parsers read (GetKeysStream, which the RSA and
ECC keys use as well) now has the carriage returns taken out.
---
 src/core/hw/aes/key.cpp | 7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/core/hw/aes/key.cpp b/src/core/hw/aes/key.cpp
index 2c7fe1341..3df7ddb5b 100644
--- a/src/core/hw/aes/key.cpp
+++ b/src/core/hw/aes/key.cpp
@@ -299,7 +299,12 @@ std::istringstream GetKeysStream() {
     std::ifstream file(filepath, std::ios_base::in | std::ios_base::binary);
     std::istringstream ret;
     if (file.is_open()) {
-        return std::istringstream(std::string(std::istreambuf_iterator<char>(file), {}));
+        // PS5: without the carriage returns of a file saved with Windows line endings, which the
+        // parsers' std::getline would keep: ":AES\r" is no section they know, so not one key
+        // loaded (the RSA and ECC sections read this stream too)
+        std::string text(std::istreambuf_iterator<char>(file), {});
+        std::erase(text, '\r');
+        return std::istringstream(std::move(text));
     } else {
 #ifdef ENABLE_BUILTIN_KEYBLOB
         // The key data is encrypted in the source to prevent easy access to it for unintended
